1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 c5 5. Bd3 d5 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O Nc6 8. a3 Bxc3 9. bxc3 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Qc7 11. Bb5 b6 12. Re1 Rd8 13. Bb2 Bb7 14. e4 Qf4 15. e5 Nd5 16. Bd3 f5 17. exf6 Qxf6 18. Qc2 cxd4 19. Bxh7+ Kh8 20. cxd4 Nce7 21. Be4 Nf5 22. Bc1 Rdc8 23. Qb2 Kg8 24. Bg5 Qf7 25. Ne5 Qh5 26. Qd2 Qe8 27. a4 Rc7 28. g4 Nd6 29. Bg6 Qc8 30. Rac1 Rc3 31. Be7 Nf4 32. Bh7+ {Source: CHESS, November 7th 1959, Vol.25/351, p44. "Perhaps the most interesting game of the match was the first round game of Donner-Clarke. This was a "normal" variation of the Nimzo-Indian. which should have favoured Clarke as he has scored some fine victories with this opening. However, Clarke told Barden afterwards that he had played like a rabbit, to which Barden replied, after considering some of Clarke's more attacking moves. "Yes, but perhaps an aggressive rabbit." (columnist DG Horseman, Coventry Evening Telegraph, 31 October 1959, page 7)} 1-0

